  "transcript": "# Entrepreneurship and business\n\n## Headline: more people want to start, but many drop out\n> UK entrepreneurial intent is at a 25-year high, but many startups fail to survive or scale.\n\nChair: Mark Hart, the Global Entrepreneurship Monitor came out today—what are the headlines for the UK?\nProfessor Hart: This is our 27th year. The big point is that entrepreneurial ambition is at its highest since 1999.\nProfessor Hart: We measure people thinking of starting, those in their first months, and established owners. About 46% of adults now fall into one of those categories.\nProfessor Hart: Nascent and new entrepreneurs—those in the first 2½ years—are about 16% of adults, up from roughly 8% 25 years ago. That's a doubling.\nChair: So that sounds positive. Is it all good news?\nProfessor Hart: Not entirely. We are creating founders but not keeping them. Exits are rising. Women, for example, drop out of their startups at twice the rate of men.\n\n\n## Growth: very few businesses scale\n> Only a small share of startups reach substantial turnover or rapid growth.\n\nChair: You mentioned survival. How many firms hit the £1 million turnover mark in three years?\nProfessor Hart: Using HMRC and ONS data, only around 2% of each startup cohort reaches £1 million in three years; half of the rest fold or stay small. The median turnover is about £84,000.\nAlison Griffiths: Internationally, how do we compare on starting businesses and survival?\nProfessor Hart: We sit between the US and mainland Europe—more entrepreneurial than France and Germany on start-up rates, less than the US. Survival rates at firm level are broadly similar to France and Germany.\nAlison Griffiths: If we've got high start-up intent, can better support push more of them to scale?\nProfessor Hart: Yes. France and Germany run coordinated youth and university support programmes that improve survival. If we matched that support for our more entrepreneurial population, we'd likely see better outcomes.\n\n\n## Education, geography and targeted youth support\n> Weak entrepreneurship education and uneven local support hold back founders; a youth stipend programme is a clear option.\n\nDan Aldridge: Which framework condition is holding the UK back most?\nProfessor Hart: Entrepreneurship education at school and university, and awareness of existing finance. We have funds, but people don't know how to access them.\nDan Aldridge: Do the data show regional gaps? My town struggles with underfunded local support.\nProfessor Hart: Yes—big regional and local disparities. Access has to be improved everywhere, not just cities.\nAntonia Bance: What could the Budget do tomorrow to encourage starts?\nProfessor Hart: We should fund youth enterprise properly—monthly stipends and capital to let young founders prove ideas. Look at Germany's system: monthly support of €1,000–€2,000 and access to capital like €30,000.\nAntonia Bance: Would that be universal? What eligibility?\nProfessor Hart: Not universal. Programs pick people with growth ambition or university projects—roughly tens of thousands of participants, not every student.\nPhilip Salter: We also suggested bringing back enterprise allowances and extending the Start-Up Loans focus so self-employed founders could qualify for employer-style hiring grants, since new firms often go on to hire people.\n\n\n## Talent, visas and barriers for ethnic minority founders\n> Foreign-born founders and ethnic minority businesses are important to UK growth but face visa uncertainty, finance gaps and procurement barriers.\n\nMr Reynolds: Phil, your Job Creators report says many top-growth firms have foreign-born founders. Does visa uncertainty deter them?\nPhilip Salter: Yes. Over half of the fastest-growing firms have at least one foreign-born founder. But visa rules are uncertain and costly, deterring people from committing long term.\nPhilip Salter: We lack a functioning entrepreneur visa and could broaden the high-potential individual visa to include business schools. Visa fees for founders and dependants are expensive compared with Europe.\nChair: Professor Ram, what are the barriers ethnic minority entrepreneurs face?\nProfessor Ram: Ethnic minority firms contribute about £25 billion now but could reach £100 billion if they grew at the same rate as white-led firms—so a £75 billion opportunity is being missed.\nProfessor Ram: Key issues: poor data on minority entrepreneurs, harder access to finance, discouragement (people don't apply because they expect poor treatment), and weak access to procurement networks.\nProfessor Ram: Public and corporate procurement can be used to open supply chains to diverse suppliers. Local high streets—like Birmingham's Soho Road—are hubs of immigrant entrepreneurship that provide jobs and social integration, yet aren't recognised in growth policy.\nPhilip Salter: Beyond visas, there's a culture point: people choose where to build families. If the immigration path to settled life is uncertain, founders choose elsewhere.\n\n\n## Women founders, finance gaps and Budget asks\n> Women are starting businesses in record numbers but receive a tiny share of equity; witnesses propose targeted early-stage funds, more women angel investors, tax changes, and data fixes.\n\nSarah Edwards: GEM shows record early-stage female entrepreneurship but women drop out around years two to three. What can the Government do?\nJordan Dargue: If women got equal access to direct finance, the economy could gain over £300 billion. Investment into women rose, but women-led firms still receive only about 2% of equity funding.\nJordan Dargue: We need funding products beyond debt—more equity, more women angels, and regional spread of female angel investors. We propose a pound-for-pound public co-investment for women angel investors to scale early-stage equity.\nChair: How should public funds such as the National Wealth Fund or British Business Bank be used?\nJordan Dargue: Those funds currently back growth and scale-stage deals. We need some of that money ringfenced for early-stage and especially for women-led and regional opportunities.\nJordan Dargue: Also stabilise tax-efficient investment schemes and consider enhancements for women backing women—that would mobilise wealth transfers and increase female investor numbers.\nPhilip Salter: Fix R&D tax credits. Many innovative small firms avoid claiming because past mismanagement led HMRC to hound legitimate claimants. Properly targeted credits boost innovation.\nPhilip Salter: And invest in government data plumbing: a single business identifier across departments would save time and let government target help to fast-growing firms, female founders in Doncaster, etc.\nProfessor Ram: Two rapid Budget asks: make sure the Business Growth Network is tasked and measured on supporting female and ethnic minority firms, and create an accountable body inside government to drive inclusion across policies.\nChair: Thank you. Those are clear, testable asks for tomorrow's Budget and for the Committee to follow up.\n",
